**Endianness** is the order in which a multi-byte value is stored in memory. For `0x12345678` at address `A`:
- **Little-endian** (x86, most ARM setups): least significant byte first: `78 56 34 12`.
- **Big-endian** (network byte order, some older CPUs): most significant byte first: `12 34 56 78`.
It matters when exchanging binary data between machines (use `htonl`/`ntohl`), in file formats and with hardware registers.

**Detection by inspecting the first byte.**

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int main(void) {
    uint32_t x = 1;
    unsigned char *p = (unsigned char *)&x;   // char pointers may alias any object
    printf("%s endian\n", p[0] == 1 ? "Little" : "Big");
}
```
If the lowest-address byte holds `1`, the least significant byte is stored first, so the machine is little-endian. (A `union` of `uint32_t` and `uint8_t[4]` also works in C.)

**Follow-up: content at a 32-bit address.** Cast the address to a pointer and dereference it.

```c
uint32_t addr = 0x20000000;                    // example address given by the interviewer
volatile uint32_t *ptr = (volatile uint32_t *)(uintptr_t)addr;
printf("0x%08X\n", (unsigned)*ptr);
```
Points to say:
- Use `volatile` if it is a memory-mapped register so the read is not optimised away.
- On a hosted OS (Linux, Windows) an arbitrary address will usually cause a segmentation fault because of virtual memory; this only makes sense on bare-metal or embedded targets, or for an address you own (for example `&x`).
- Use `uintptr_t` for the integer-to-pointer cast, and mind alignment: a 32-bit read should be 4-byte aligned on many CPUs.
- Print the bytes individually if you want to show the byte order of the stored value.
